1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,490
Press a key on this machine, and a lamp lights up another letter.

2
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:07,610
Press the same key again, and a different lamp lights.

3
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:13,410
In the war, the army's version could be set up in nearly 159 quintillion ways.

4
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:17,520
But it had one odd habit. It never turned a letter into itself.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:23,780
That small rule, and the people who saw what it meant, helped break Enigma.

6
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:33,330
Enigma has 26 keys and 26 lamps. One operator types; another writes down the lamps that light.

7
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:39,820
Let's follow one key press. First, the key turns the right-hand rotor one step, before any current flows.

8
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,980
Then the current runs to the plugboard, where a cable can swap it for another letter.

9
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:48,150
Next come three rotors, each wired inside to turn one letter into another.

10
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:52,510
At the far end, the reflector sends the current back through the rotors by another route.

11
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:58,700
Back through the plugboard, and one lamp lights. Here, A became C.

12
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:03,980
Press A again. The rotor has moved, so every wire has shifted, and A becomes N.

13
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:09,550
Once per turn, a notch lets the right rotor push the middle one along, like a car's odometer.

14
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:14,600
Almost. The middle rotor sometimes steps twice in a row, which is called the double step.

15
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:21,050
The rotors come back to where they started only after 16,900 key presses.

16
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,860
The reflector joins the 26 letters in 13 pairs, and turns the current round.

17
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:34,310
So the machine works both ways. At the same setting, press C, and the current runs backwards to A.

18
00:01:34,390 --> 00:01:40,890
The receiver sets up a machine the same way, types in the ciphertext, and out comes the message.

19
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:44,360
But there's a price. Could a key ever light its own lamp?

20
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,740
Only if the current came back along the very wire it went in on.

21
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:51,580
That would mean the reflector joining a letter to itself, and it never does.

22
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:58,240
So no letter is ever enciphered as itself. Press A, and you get any letter but A.

23
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:03,670
We tried every key at every rotor position. Not one lit its own lamp.

24
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:09,190
The plugboard can't change that, because it swaps a letter on the way in and swaps it back on the way out.

25
00:02:11,790 --> 00:02:17,860
How many ways could the army's Enigma be set up, with three rotors from a box of five and ten cables?

26
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:23,190
Choosing the rotors and their order gives 5 × 4 × 3, which is 60 ways.

27
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:30,630
Their starting positions give 26 × 26 × 26, which is 17,576.

28
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:36,210
Ten cables pairing up letters on the plugboard give about 151 trillion ways.

29
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:43,850
Multiply them: nearly 159 quintillion settings, even before the rotors' ring settings.

30
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:49,040
At a million settings a second, trying them all would take about five million years.

31
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:56,920
But nobody broke Enigma by trying every setting. They found shortcuts, and the first came from Poland.

32
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:06,270
In 1929, Poland's Cipher Bureau trained German-speaking mathematics students in Poznań, in secret.

33
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:13,480
In September 1932, the Bureau took on three of them, Marian Rejewski, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski.

34
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:20,920
Rejewski spotted a German habit. Each message began with its own rotor setting, typed twice, from a start shared that day.

35
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,900
So the 1st and 4th letters hid the same letter, and so did the 2nd and 5th, and the 3rd and 6th.

36
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:31,970
From a day's messages, those links join up into loops, which come in pairs of the same length.

37
00:03:32,050 --> 00:03:37,380
And the plugboard changes which letters sit in each loop, but never how long the loops are.

38
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:43,560
A cryptology scholar later called this fact about permutations "the theorem that won World War Two."

39
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,570
Rejewski still needed the rotors' wiring. Help came from a spy.

40
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:55,780
Hans-Thilo Schmidt, at Germany's Cipher Office, sold secrets to the French, including two months of keys from 1932.

41
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:58,720
In December, the French shared them with Poland.

42
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,590
Another puzzle was the keys' wiring. Rejewski guessed plain order, A to A and B to B, and was right.

43
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:12,360
By the end of 1932, he had all three rotors and the reflector, without ever seeing the machine.

44
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:22,800
From January 1933, the Poles read Enigma. As the Germans tightened their rules, they kept finding new ways in.

45
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:28,830
In September 1938, each operator began choosing his own starting position, but still typed the key twice.

46
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:34,370
Sometimes a repeated letter came out the same twice. Zygalski punched sheets with holes wherever that could happen.

47
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:40,120
Stack the sheets for a day's messages, line them up, and fewer and fewer holes shine through.

48
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:46,890
Here are seven, from a simulated day. The one hole left gives away the ring settings.

49
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:53,810
Rejewski also designed the bomba, which was in effect six Enigmas wired together and driven by a motor.

50
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:58,910
Six were built, one for each order of the rotors. A day's key took about two hours.

51
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:06,160
Then, in December 1938, the Germans added two more rotors. Three from five made 60 orders, not six.

52
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:11,290
Sixty bombas would have cost fifteen times the Bureau's yearly budget for equipment.

53
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:19,650
So in late July 1939, at a secret site outside Warsaw, the Poles showed French and British codebreakers everything.

54
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:24,150
Five weeks later, Germany invaded Poland.

55
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:32,690
At Bletchley Park, Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman reported for work the day after Britain declared war.

56
00:05:32,770 --> 00:05:38,510
Turing expected the Germans to stop typing the key twice. In May 1940, they did.

57
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:44,110
So Bletchley leaned on cribs, which were stretches of a message they could guess, like set phrases.

58
00:05:44,190 --> 00:05:49,330
One outpost in North Africa regularly sent a phrase meaning nothing special to report.

59
00:05:49,410 --> 00:05:53,970
But where in the message is it? Slide the crib along the ciphertext, one place at a time.

60
00:05:54,050 --> 00:06:00,080
Enigma never enciphers a letter as itself, so wherever a crib letter sits on the same letter, the crib can't be there.

61
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:04,260
In the second place, an R sits on an R, so that place is crossed out.

62
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:08,530
Slide on, and cross out every clash.

63
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:16,500
Of these 16 places, 9 are ruled out, without any machine at all.

64
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:25,450
A crib in the right place still doesn't give you the key. For that, Turing designed the bombe.

65
00:06:25,530 --> 00:06:28,910
Line the crib up with the ciphertext, and each pair of letters is a link.

66
00:06:28,990 --> 00:06:35,930
Draw all the links, and you get a menu. Where links close into loops, the menu gets powerful.

67
00:06:36,010 --> 00:06:40,960
At each rotor position, the bombe guesses one letter's plug partner and follows the consequences.

68
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:45,760
A contradiction means that position is impossible, and the drums turn on.

69
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:52,820
Gordon Welchman added the diagonal board, which uses the rule that if A is plugged to B, B is plugged to A.

70
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:58,620
We ran our menu through every position. Without the diagonal board, it stopped 662 times.

71
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:05,640
With it, it stopped just twice. One stop was false. The other was right, and showed E plugged to J.

72
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:11,770
The first bombe, Victory, arrived in March 1940, built by Harold Keen's engineers.

73
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:18,800
By 1945 there were about two hundred, run by some two thousand Wrens of the Royal Navy.

74
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:26,560
The hardest Enigma was the navy's, with three rotors from eight and stricter procedures.

75
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:32,560
Turing took it on, he said, because no one else was doing anything about it, and he could have it to himself.

76
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:39,920
It also took captured papers. In May 1941, sailors seized codebooks and keys from the submarine U-110.

77
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,680
Through late 1941, decrypts helped steer convoys around the waiting U-boats.

78
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:51,290
Then, on 1 February 1942, the U-boats switched to a new Enigma with four rotors.

79
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:56,640
For ten months, Bletchley couldn't read them, and the wolf packs ravaged convoys.

80
00:07:56,720 --> 00:08:06,020
In October 1942, off Port Said, sailors from HMS Petard climbed into the sinking U-559 for its papers.

81
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:11,060
Two of them, Tony Fasson and Colin Grazier, were still inside when it sank, and drowned.

82
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:15,460
By December 1942, U-boat messages were being read again.

83
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:21,960
How much this decided the Atlantic is still argued. Radar and much else mattered too.

84
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:30,490
In the film The Imitation Game, Turing builds one machine almost alone, and calls it Christopher.

85
00:08:30,570 --> 00:08:35,660
In fact, the first bombe was Victory, and breaking Enigma took thousands of people.

86
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:43,230
By January 1945, Bletchley Park and its outstations had almost 9,000 staff. About three quarters were women.

87
00:08:43,310 --> 00:08:50,260
Harry Hinsley, a veteran and later Bletchley's official historian, judged their work shortened the war by at least two years.

88
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:56,310
Other historians find any such number too precise. No one can run a war twice.

89
00:08:56,390 --> 00:09:04,540
One of the last messages Bletchley decoded was sent from Cuxhaven on 7 May 1945. British troops had entered the town.

90
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:10,640
The operator signed off: "Closing down forever. All the best. Goodbye."

91
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:16,840
The reflector made Enigma convenient. It also meant no letter could ever be itself.

92
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:21,890
Add German habits, captured papers and thousands of patient people, and that was enough.

93
00:09:21,970 --> 00:09:26,480
Press A, and you could get any letter at all, except A.
